  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (2.5 x^2 + 0.5 z^2) / 2, drawn at 0.4 times its value. Each update is x <- x - rate (slope + noise). Across (x) the step overshoots once rate > 0.8 (=2/2.5), so swings grow toward the top of the range.",
    "Noise on each slope component is random with spread 1.5 * sqrt((64 - b) / (63 b)) for batch b: largest at 1, exactly zero at 64. It comes from a fixed seed, run number and step, so Reset replays the same noise from the start.",
    "Both arrows show steps (rate times slope estimate) drawn at 3 times their true length, the same scale for both. Purple: the mini-batch step the ball will take. Dark: the step the full-data slope gives.",
    "A run finishes after 40 steps. It is marked diverged as soon as the loss goes above 5, the loss at the bowl's steep edge. Off-bowl points are drawn at the rim, and the Loss readout keeps the true value.",
    "The running ball takes 5 steps per second at speed 1. The trail marks every step. The ghost keeps the last run's path, up to 41 points including the start."
  ]
}